The written agreement must contain:  
(a) the rights and liabilities of each party;  
(b) the fee to be charged;  
(c) the client's cooperation for periodic review;  
(d) the client's obligation to provide true, adequate, and timely information;  
(e) the CRA's obligation to disclose the rating assigned, regardless of acceptance by the client;  
(f) the client's obligation to disclose all ratings assigned in the last three years and any unaccepted rating.

A credit rating agency must maintain records for a minimum period of five years. The records include:  
(a) copy of its balance sheet;  
(b) copy of its profit and loss account;  
(c) copy of the auditor's report;  
(d) copy of the agreement with each client;  
(e) information supplied by each client;  
(f) correspondence with each client;  
(g) ratings assigned, including upgradation and downgradation;  
(h) rating notes considered by the rating committee;  
(i) record of decisions of the rating committee;  
(j) letter assigning rating;  
(k) particulars of fees charged.

The target company's board of directors is prohibited from the following actions during the offer period without shareholder approval by postal ballot:

(a) Alienating any material assets outside the ordinary course of business;  
(b) Effecting any material borrowings outside the ordinary course of business;  
(c) Issuing or allotting any authorised but unissued securities entitling voting rights;  
(d) Implementing any buy-back or effecting capital structure changes;  
(e) Entering into, amending or terminating material contracts outside the ordinary course;  
(f) Accelerating any contingent vesting of rights including ESOPs.

The three personal qualifications that every trustee of a mutual fund must possess or satisfy under Regulation 16(2) are:  
(a) being a person of ability, integrity, and standing;  
(b) not being found guilty of moral turpitude;  
(c) not being convicted of any economic offence or violation of any securities laws.
